          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I smoke and I have been diagnosed with COPD.",
          "answer": "Quitting smoking is the most important step you can take with COPD. According to the COPD guidance (Global respiratory strategy report, educational summary), smoking cessation is the only intervention shown to change how quickly lung function declines. Stopping does not reverse the damage that has already happened, but it can slow further loss.\n\nThe same guidance says pulmonary rehabilitation improves breathlessness and exercise ability more than inhalers do, and that vaccines against influenza, pneumococcal disease, COVID-19 and, where recommended, RSV reduce flare-ups. Your clinician can tell you which of these apply to you.\n\nThe coronary artery guidance also notes that combining behavioural support with medication works better than advice alone. That passage is about heart disease, but the general point about support is relevant. The results I received did not include the specific quitting medicines or support programmes for people with COPD, so please ask your doctor, pharmacist or a local stop-smoking service what is available and suitable for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
