          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does stopping actually change anything at this stage?",
          "answer": "I could not find a passage that directly answers whether stopping smoking still makes a difference after a COPD diagnosis. The results I received came from general reference summaries on COPD, asthma, diabetes, reflux and thyroid disease. The COPD summary (General clinical reference, educational summary) says that tobacco smoking is the dominant cause of COPD in high-income countries. It does not say what quitting does at a later stage.\n\nMy earlier answer drew on the Global respiratory strategy report, which said that stopping smoking is the only intervention shown to change how quickly lung function declines. That statement was in my earlier answer, but it did not appear in the passages returned for this question, so I cannot re-check it here. Please read that report yourself or ask your doctor.\n\nYour clinician is the best person to ask whether stopping now would help you specifically. They can also talk you through support options.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
